WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.388
A strong validation strategy starts by
defining what valid input looks like.

2
00:00:04.468 --> 00:00:08.297
Instead of trying to list every possible
invalid case, describe the source,

3
00:00:08.377 --> 00:00:12.146
type, required fields, allowed characters,
length limits, range limits,

4
00:00:12.226 --> 00:00:15.815
encoding, and state relationships that the
program actually expects.

5
00:00:15.895 --> 00:00:19.724
Accept-known-good validation is usually
easier to reason about than broad

6
00:00:19.804 --> 00:00:20.462
acceptance.

7
00:00:20.542 --> 00:00:24.251
An allowlist says which forms are
permitted and rejects everything else.

8
00:00:24.331 --> 00:00:27.621
That is clearer than relying on a long
denylist of unusual values,

9
00:00:27.701 --> 00:00:31.290
especially when the input space includes
many encodings, separators,

10
00:00:31.370 --> 00:00:33.942
control characters, or partially formed
records.

11
00:00:34.022 --> 00:00:37.671
Ambiguous data should be rejected or
normalized only under clear rules.

12
00:00:37.751 --> 00:00:41.520
For example, the program should know
whether extra whitespace is allowed,

13
00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:45.847
whether repeated delimiters are
meaningful, whether case changes are acceptable,

14
00:00:45.927 --> 00:00:49.457
and whether more than one representation
of the same value can appear.

15
00:00:49.537 --> 00:00:53.425
Canonical forms help only when the
canonicalization rule itself is precise.

16
00:00:53.505 --> 00:00:57.274
Validation should be close to the boundary
where input enters the program.

17
00:00:57.354 --> 00:01:00.465
If raw input travels through many layers
before it is checked,

18
00:01:00.545 --> 00:01:03.596
more code has to defend itself against
every possible shape.

19
00:01:03.676 --> 00:01:07.744
A validated internal representation lets
later code work with clearer types and

20
00:01:07.824 --> 00:01:08.960
smaller assumptions.

21
00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:11.014
The strategy should match the use case.

22
00:01:11.094 --> 00:01:14.983
A parser for a configuration file, a
command-line option, a field in a record,

23
00:01:15.063 --> 00:01:17.575
and a device message may all need
different rules.

24
00:01:17.655 --> 00:01:21.843
The shared discipline is to define
expected good input and reject values that do not

25
00:01:21.923 --> 00:01:22.820
fit the contract.
